nd g1(π) = x2, gk(π) = gk−1(π̃) for k ≥ 2.\n\nIn both cases, no crossings are introduced. Assuming inductively that ℓ(π̃) = A(x̃) and g(π̃) = dep(B(x̃)), one can check that ℓ(π) = A(x) and g(π) = dep(B(x)).\n\nWe restate Proposition 8.8 as a sum over compositions using Proposition 8.18:\n\nCorollary 8.20. Let (x̄, ȳ) be a totally positive specialization expressed in terms of the sequences t̄ and c̄ as in (4.8); see Proposition 4.4. Then the associated n-th Stieltjes moment is given by\n\na_n =  ∑_{x ∈ Comp(n)} N(x) c^{A(x)} t^{dep(B(x))}  (8.9)\n\nwhere N(x) = #{π ∈ NC(n) : ζ(π) = x}, and where A(x) and dep(B(x)) are given in Definitions 8.12 and 8.15.\n\n8.5. Multiplicity matrix\n\nLet us discuss the multiplicities N(x) appearing in Equation (8.9). First, by Proposition 8.18, this multiplicity is nonzero for all x ∈ Comp(n). Thus, there are 2^{n−1} summands in (8.9) all of which are positive. Since\n\nCatalan(n) = #NC(n) =  ∑_{x ∈ Comp(n)} N(x),\n\nthe Stieltjes moments a_n may be thought of as defining a multivariate extension of the Catalan numbers in the parameters c̄ and t̄. If we specialize c_k = 1 (or t_k = 1) for all k ≥ 1, then the polynomial expression for a_n in (8.9) collapses to a sum of F_n distinct monomials, each with a positive integer coefficient, where F_n is the n-th Fibonacci number. This observation leads us to the following construction.\n\nDefinition 8.21 (Multiplicity matrix). For a pair of Fibonacci words u, v ∈ YF_n, set N_{u,v} = N(x) if u = u(x) and v = v(x) for some composition x ∈ Comp(n). By Lemma 8.14, this composition is unique\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1017/fms.2025.10165 Published online by Cambridge University Press--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLucas Mol, Narad Rampersad and Jeffrey Shallit (https://cm.episciences.org/14019/pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; We also offer the following conjecture concerning the Dyck factors of the paperfolding seque